\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{Sweave}
\begin{document}

\title{Introduction to the Rhpc Package (English)}
\author{Ei-ji Nakama}
\maketitle

\section{Overview}
The \texttt{Rhpc} package provides a high-performance computing (HPC) interface for R that leverages the Message Passing Interface (MPI) to execute parallel computations efficiently. Unlike standard parallel libraries that often rely on sockets or forking, \texttt{Rhpc} offers a direct, low-latency interface to MPI, making it suitable for both small multicore systems and larger distributed HPC clusters.

\subsection{Key Specifications}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{MPI-Specific API}: All functions are prefixed with \texttt{Rhpc\_} to avoid namespace collisions and clearly indicate MPI-backed operations (e.g., \texttt{Rhpc\_lapply}, \texttt{Rhpc\_worker\_call}).
    \item \textbf{Dynamic Worker Management}: Supports both static allocation (via \texttt{mpirun}) and dynamic process spawning during runtime.
    \item \textbf{Hybrid Communication}: Utilizes Collective communication for instruction broadcasting and Point-to-Point communication for result gathering to maximize throughput.
    \item \textbf{Transparent Windows Support}: Includes a unique \texttt{fakemaster} mechanism to allow MPI usage within GUI environments like RStudio without complex command-line invocation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Basic Usage}
To use \texttt{Rhpc}, initialize the MPI environment first and then obtain a worker cluster handle. The behavior of \texttt{Rhpc\_getHandle()} depends on how it is called.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{No argument}: obtains an existing worker process when R is already running under \texttt{mpirun} or \texttt{mpiexec}.
    \item \textbf{Numeric argument}: dynamically spawns the specified number of worker processes.
\end{itemize}

The following example shows how to initialize the system and retrieve the process IDs (PIDs) from all active workers.

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
library(Rhpc)
# Initialize the Rhpc environment
Rhpc_initialize()

# Obtain a cluster handle (no argument when running under mpirun/mpiexec,
# or specify a number like Rhpc_getHandle(4) to spawn workers dynamically)
cl <- Rhpc_getHandle()

# Execute a command on all workers and collect results on the master process
pids <- Rhpc_worker_call(cl, Sys.getpid)
print(pids)

# If you want to run code on workers without collecting results,
# use Rhpc_worker_noback(). This is useful when you only need execution.
Rhpc_worker_noback(cl, function() {
    cat("worker process:", Sys.getpid(), "\n")
})

# Finalize the Rhpc environment
Rhpc_finalize()
@

\section{Backend Management on Windows}
On Windows, \texttt{Rhpc} greatly simplifies MPI environment setup. In GUI environments such as RGui or RStudio, it is not necessary to start R with \texttt{mpiexec} from the beginning. Instead, \texttt{Rhpc} uses a helper program called \texttt{fakemaster} to prepare the MPI master environment.

When \texttt{Rhpc\_initialize()} is called, it performs the following steps internally:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If the current R process is not running under \texttt{mpiexec}, start \texttt{fakemaster}.
    \item Launch \texttt{fakemaster.exe} via \texttt{mpiexec} and establish a connection between \texttt{fakemaster} and the current R process using a named pipe.
    \item Read the MPI environment variables generated by \texttt{fakemaster} and import them into the current R process.
    \item This allows MPI operations such as \texttt{Rhpc\_getHandle()} and \texttt{Rhpc\_worker\_call()} to work in a GUI-based R session.
    \item It also enables dynamic worker creation through \texttt{Rhpc\_getHandle()}.
\end{enumerate}

With this mechanism, a Windows R session does not have to be started directly with \texttt{mpiexec}. Simply calling \texttt{Rhpc\_initialize()} is enough, and the details of starting and connecting \texttt{mpiexec} and \texttt{fakemaster} are handled automatically.

Note the following points:
\begin{itemize}
    \item When \texttt{Rhpc\_initialize()} is executed, \texttt{mpiexec} and \texttt{fakemaster} windows are launched in the background.
    \item Closing these windows or processes prematurely will break the MPI connection and cause \texttt{Rhpc} operations to fail.
    \item If needed, you can specify the \texttt{mpiexec} command line, for example with \texttt{options(Rhpc.mpiexec="mpiexec -n 1")}. 
    \item On Windows, MS-MPI must be installed, and the environment variables \texttt{MSMPI\_INC}, \texttt{MSMPI\_LIB32}, and \texttt{MSMPI\_LIB64} need to be configured.
\end{itemize}

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
library(Rhpc)
# Initialize Rhpc. On Windows, this starts mpiexec and fakemaster automatically
# and sets up the MPI master environment.
Rhpc_initialize()
cl <- Rhpc_getHandle()

# Retrieve environment information from each worker to verify setup.
# This confirms that the workers are active and the environment is configured correctly.
worker_env_info <- Rhpc_worker_call(cl, function() {
    list(
        computer_name = Sys.getenv("COMPUTERNAME"),
        username = Sys.getenv("USERNAME")
    )
})
print(worker_env_info)

# Finalize the Rhpc environment
Rhpc_finalize()
@

\section{Rhpc MPI Backend and Communication Model}
\texttt{Rhpc} combines MPI collective communication with per-worker point-to-point communication to efficiently gather function execution results.

\subsection{Common processing: instruction distribution with \texttt{MPI\_Bcast}}
Many \texttt{Rhpc} functions serialize the target function and its arguments on the master process only once and broadcast them to all workers using \texttt{MPI\_Bcast}. Unlike standard R parallel backends that send data to each worker sequentially ($O(N)$), \texttt{MPI\_Bcast} leverages optimized tree-based distribution algorithms, typically achieving $O(\log N)$ performance. In the backend of functions such as \texttt{Rhpc\_worker\_call()} and \texttt{Rhpc\_lapply()}, this significantly reduces the CPU and memory pressure on the master process.

\subsection{Result collection and individual communication}
When results are collected, each worker sends its execution result back to the master individually. Workers send data asynchronously using \texttt{MPI\_Isend}, while the master initiates non-blocking receives from each worker with \texttt{MPI\_Irecv} and waits for all transfers to complete with \texttt{MPI\_Waitall}.

This asynchronous model allows the communication to overlap with the serialization/deserialization tasks, preventing a slow worker from blocking the master's ability to receive data from other faster workers.

This approach offers the following advantages:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Input data and instructions intended for all workers are sent once using collective communication, avoiding repeated transmission.
    \item Because output sizes often differ among workers, result reception is handled flexibly by point-to-point communication.
    \item Results can be aggregated efficiently as they arrive, without strict dependency on the completion order of workers.
    \item Reduced serialization overhead on the master side, as the common function logic is only serialized once for the broadcast.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Simplified communication when results are not required}
Functions such as \texttt{Rhpc\_worker\_noback()} and some export operations only broadcast instructions from the master to all workers. Since no results are returned, communication can be completed with \texttt{MPI\_Bcast} alone, and point-to-point communication is unnecessary.

\subsection{Dynamic worker creation and \texttt{MPI\_Comm\_spawn}}
If the current process is not already running under \texttt{mpiexec}, \texttt{Rhpc\_initialize()} calls \texttt{MPI\_Comm\_spawn} internally to create worker processes dynamically. On Windows, \texttt{fakemaster} precedes this step by launching \texttt{mpiexec} and passing MPI environment variables into the current R process. As a result, worker processes can be created dynamically even in GUI environments, and the collective/individual communication model can be used.

\section{Communication Diagram (sequence diagram)}
The following R plot illustrates, in a simple sequence-style diagram, how collective communication
(e.g. \texttt{MPI\_Bcast}) and point-to-point communication (e.g. \texttt{MPI\_Isend}/\texttt{MPI\_Irecv})
are used by \texttt{Rhpc} functions. This is a rough schematic drawn using base R plotting primitives.

<<echo=TRUE, fig=TRUE, width=6, height=4>>=
# Sequence-style diagram: Master vs multiple Workers
draw_rhpc_sequence <- function(nworkers=3){
    lanes <- c("Master", paste0("Worker", seq_len(nworkers)))
    nl <- length(lanes)
    y <- rev(seq_len(nl))
    y_master <- y[1]

    oldpar <- par(mar=c(3,2,3,2))
    on.exit(par(oldpar))
    plot(c(0,10), c(0.5, nl+0.5), type="n", xaxt="n", yaxt="n", xlab="Time", ylab="",
             main="Collective vs Point-to-Point Communication (schematic)")
    # draw lifelines
    for(i in seq_along(lanes)){
        text(0.4, y[i], lanes[i], adj=0)
        segments(1, y[i], 9.5, y[i], lty=2, col="#777777")
    }

    # collective: MPI_Bcast at t=2 (draw arrows from master to all workers)
    text(2, nl+0.2, "MPI_Bcast (broadcast input)", cex=0.9)
    for(i in 2:length(y)){
        arrows(2, y_master, 2.9, y[i], length=0.06, code=2)
    }

    # point-to-point example: master -> worker2, then worker2 -> master
    w2 <- 3 # show as Worker2 (index into lanes)
    x1 <- 4; x2 <- 5
    arrows(x1, y_master, x2, y[w2], col="#1f77b4", length=0.06)
    text((x1+x2)/2, (y_master+y[w2])/2 + 0.25, "MPI_Isend (to one worker)", col="#1f77b4", cex=0.8)

    # reply
    x3 <- 6; x4 <- 7
    arrows(x3, y[w2], x4, y_master, col="#d62728", length=0.06)
    text((x3+x4)/2, (y_master+y[w2])/2 - 0.25, "MPI_Isend / MPI_Irecv (reply)", col="#d62728", cex=0.8)

    legend(8.1, nl+0.4, legend=c("collective (MPI_Bcast)", "p2p (MPI_Isend/MPI_Irecv)"),
                 col=c("black","#1f77b4"), lty=1, bty="n", cex=0.8)
}

draw_rhpc_sequence(nworkers=3)
@

\section{Parallel Mapping with \texttt{Rhpc\_lapply}}
One of the most powerful features of \texttt{Rhpc} is parallel mapping over lists and vectors with \texttt{Rhpc\_lapply}. This function preserves the behavior of base \texttt{lapply} while distributing work across initialized MPI workers. By default, it chunks the input data and distributes it to workers.

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
Rhpc_initialize()
cl <- Rhpc_getHandle()

# Perform parallel computation
input_data <- 1:10
results <- Rhpc_lapply(cl, input_data, function(x) {
    return(x^2)
})
print(unlist(results))
Rhpc_finalize()
@

\section{Load Balancing with \texttt{Rhpc\_lapplyLB}}
For tasks where the execution time varies significantly between elements, \texttt{Rhpc\_lapplyLB} provides a Load Balancing mechanism. Instead of pre-chunking the data, it distributes tasks to workers as they become available, ensuring higher CPU utilization in heterogeneous workloads.

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
Rhpc_initialize()
cl <- Rhpc_getHandle()
input_data <- 1:10

# Load balanced version for uneven task lengths
results_lb <- Rhpc_lapplyLB(cl, input_data, function(x) {
    Sys.sleep(runif(1, 0, 1)) # Simulate variable workload
    return(x * 10)
})
Rhpc_finalize()
@

\section{Exporting Variables to Workers}
In many cases, workers need access to data or variables defined in the master R session. The \texttt{Rhpc\_Export} function broadcasts variables to the global environment of all worker nodes.

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
multiplier <- 10
# Export the variable 'multiplier' to all workers
Rhpc_Export(cl, "multiplier")

# Use the exported variable on the worker side
res <- Rhpc_worker_call(cl, function() {
    return(multiplier * 2)
})
print(res)
Rhpc_finalize()
@

\section{Summary}
Using \texttt{Rhpc} allows R users to bridge high-level statistical programming with the performance of low-level MPI. The main advantages are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Efficiency:} communication overhead is minimized compared to socket-based parallel processing.
    \item \textbf{Scalability:} transitioning from local multicore testing to large distributed cluster execution is smooth.
    \item \textbf{Simplicity:} complex parallel tasks can be written in familiar R-style syntax.
\end{itemize}

